WEBVTT
1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.380
In the last couple of lessons, we've
looked at how we can

2
00:00:02.380 --> 00:00:06.140
use query augmentation and cross-encoder
re-ranking to improve

3
00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:07.120
retrieval results.

4
00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:10.740
In this lesson, I'm going to show
you how we can use user

5
00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:13.040
feedback about the
relevancy of retrieved

6
00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:16.600
results to automatically
improve the performance

7
00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:18.620
of the retrieval
system using a

8
00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:20.780
technique called embedding
adapters.

9
00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:22.940
Let me show you how it works.

10
00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.480
Embedding adapters are a
way to alter the

11
00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:27.320
embedding of a query
directly in order to

12
00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:30.320
produce better retrieval results.
In effect, we insert an

13
00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:32.320
additional stage in
the retrieval system

14
00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:34.580
called an embedding adapter,
which happens after

15
00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:36.320
the embedding model,
but before we retrieve

16
00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:37.540
the most relevant results.

17
00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:40.540
We train the embedding
adapter using user feedback

18
00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:43.400
on the relevancy of
our retrieved results for

19
00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:44.440
a set of queries.

20
00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:45.480
Let's get into it.

21
00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:48.440
So the first thing we do is grab
our helper functions as before.

22
00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:52.880
One special thing here is we're
going to need Torch because we're going

23
00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:55.580
to effectively train a model, but a
very lightweight one.

24
00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:57.440
And we create
our embedding function

25
00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:58.680
and load everything into Corma.

26
00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:00.960
And again,
we project all of our data.

27
00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:10.390
So the first thing we need
for this approach is a data set.

28
00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:12.890
We don't
have one ready because we haven't really

29
00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:15.170
had users using our
RAG application.

30
00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:18.390
But we can use a model
to generate a data set for us.

31
00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:20.610
And once again, this is all about
creating the right prompt.

32
00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:24.530
So we're going to use GPT again. And
essentially, we're prompting

33
00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:27.570
the model as an expert, helpful financial
research assistant. And it

34
00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:29.990
should suggest 10 to 15 short
questions that are

35
00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:33.570
important to ask when analyzing an
annual report and with some guidelines

36
00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:35.150
about what the
output should be like.

37
00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:37.810
And this will generate some queries
that users might actually have run

38
00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:38.910
against our system.

39
00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:41.210
So let's
ask it to generate those queries.

40
00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:43.510
We see that these
are fairly reasonable questions

41
00:01:43.510 --> 00:01:46.870
to ask about any company's
financial statements.

42
00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:52.270
So we're
going to get the results from Chrome.

43
00:01:52.430 --> 00:01:55.390
And what we're going to do, and we'll get
the retrieved documents associated

44
00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:56.030
with the results.

45
00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:58.110
And what we're going to do is
also ask the model

46
00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:00.930
to evaluate the results. In
a real RAG system, you

47
00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:03.110
can easily ask your
users to give a thumbs up or thumbs

48
00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:06.370
down on the generated output, and
then reference that with the retrieved

49
00:02:06.370 --> 00:02:09.190
results to give a signal about
what results were

50
00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:11.370
actually relevant and
which weren't. In this case, we don't

51
00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:14.990
quite have that, but we can
use a model to evaluate the relevancy of

52
00:02:14.990 --> 00:02:17.170
the retrieved results for
each query. And again, this

53
00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:18.390
is just
about prompting the model.

54
00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:21.310
So we're going to ask our helpful expert
financial assistant to

55
00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:24.130
tell us whether a given statement
is relevant to the given query.

56
00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:27.250
And we're going to ask it to output
only yes or no. And then

57
00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:29.390
we're going to
essentially transform yes to

58
00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:32.730
one's and no to
negative one's. And I'll explain why in just a

59
00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:35.370
minute, but that's the prompt. And then
what we're going to do

60
00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:38.730
is we are going to get our retrieved
embeddings and our query

61
00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:39.330
embeddings.

62
00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:41.870
And we're going to start making
a dataset to train

63
00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:42.810
our embedding adapter.

64
00:02:42.970 --> 00:02:44.610
And the way we're
going to do it is like this.

65
00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:46.910
We're going to have our
adapter query embeddings, adapter

66
00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:48.870
doc embeddings, and
our adapter labels.

67
00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:52.210
Now the adapter prefix just means we're
going to use this in a dataset. They're

68
00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:55.490
not special in any way. they're just
the embeddings of our queries

69
00:02:55.490 --> 00:02:58.130
and the embeddings of
our documents. The labels we're

70
00:02:58.130 --> 00:03:01.330
going to get from our evaluation model,
the label is going

71
00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:04.970
to be plus one or minus one depending
on whether the document

72
00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:07.130
is relevant or not
to the given query.

73
00:03:07.130 --> 00:03:09.650
So we're just going to loop
over everything to create these triples.

74
00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:12.210
So the model is
performing an evaluation for us.

75
00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:14.370
Now it's
no mistake that our labels are

76
00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:16.830
plus one and minus one, because
what we're going

77
00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:19.130
to do when when we're training our
embedding adapter model

78
00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:23.030
is use these
values as our loss function for

79
00:03:23.030 --> 00:03:23.670
cosine distance.

80
00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:27.230
When two vectors are identical,
the cosine similarity

81
00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:28.690
between them is one.

82
00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:31.430
When two vectors are opposite,
the cosine similarity between

83
00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:33.030
them is negative one.
In other words,

84
00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:35.230
we want relevant results
to point in the

85
00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:37.810
same direction as vectors,
and we want irrelevant results to

86
00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:39.250
point in the
opposite direction from

87
00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:40.030
a given query.

88
00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:42.350
And this is the model that we're
going to train.

89
00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:44.770
That's exactly what it's going
to try to do.

90
00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:48.710
All right, and let's
check out the length of our data set.

91
00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:51.050
Great, 150.

92
00:03:51.370 --> 00:03:54.070
So that's
15 queries with 10 results each, each

93
00:03:54.070 --> 00:03:55.730
one labeled for relevancy.

94
00:03:56.430 --> 00:03:59.150
So the next thing we need to do,
because we're using Torch to train

95
00:03:59.150 --> 00:04:00.170
our embedding adopter,

96
00:04:00.370 --> 00:04:02.150
is we need to
transform our data set

97
00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:04.810
into a Torch tensor data set.

98
00:04:05.330 --> 00:04:07.610
So we're just going to do
some data manipulation here

99
00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:10.310
to transform these into
Torch tensor types. And

100
00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:12.070
finally, we're going
to pack everything into a

101
00:04:12.070 --> 00:04:13.930
Torch data set.

102
00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:20.850
So let's
set up our embedding adapter model.

103
00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:23.090
The first thing is to
set up the model itself,

104
00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:24.810
and the model
is fairly straightforward.

105
00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:27.790
It takes as input a query embedding,
a document embedding,

106
00:04:27.790 --> 00:04:31.930
and an adapter matrix. We compute an
updated query embedding

107
00:04:31.930 --> 00:04:34.110
by multiplying our
original query embedding

108
00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:36.910
by the adapter matrix, and then we
compute the cosine

109
00:04:36.910 --> 00:04:39.230
similarity between our
updated query embedding

110
00:04:39.230 --> 00:04:42.070
and our document embedding. Next,
let's define our loss function.

111
00:04:42.290 --> 00:04:43.750
Again,
our loss takes a query embedding,

112
00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:46.750
document embedding, adapter matrix,
and label. And we run the model

113
00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:49.430
to compute the cosine similarity,
and we compute the mean squared

114
00:04:49.430 --> 00:04:51.830
error between the
cosine similarity and the label.

115
00:04:51.830 --> 00:04:54.590
And you'll notice again that
the plus one label means that

116
00:04:54.590 --> 00:04:56.670
the cosine similarity
says that the

117
00:04:56.670 --> 00:04:59.150
vectors are pointing in the same
direction, and a negative one

118
00:04:59.150 --> 00:05:01.470
label means they should be pointing
in the opposite direction. In

119
00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:04.750
this way, we want our queries to
be pointing in the

120
00:05:04.750 --> 00:05:06.630
same direction as relevant
documents, and

121
00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:08.970
in the opposite direction
to irrelevant documents. And

122
00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:11.690
this is what we're training
our adapter matrix to do. We initialize

123
00:05:11.690 --> 00:05:13.390
our adapter matrix
for training. You might

124
00:05:13.390 --> 00:05:15.210
recognize this is very
similar to a linear

125
00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:17.670
layer in a traditional
neural network. And that's

126
00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:18.610
really all we're doing.

127
00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:24.450
Next, let's
set up our training loop. We

128
00:05:24.450 --> 00:05:26.890
set our minimum loss and
our best matrix as things

129
00:05:26.890 --> 00:05:29.290
to keep track of. Let's train
for 100 epochs.

130
00:05:29.490 --> 00:05:31.190
For each query embedding,
document embedding,

131
00:05:31.190 --> 00:05:34.090
and label in our Torch data set,
we compute our loss.

132
00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:36.330
If the loss that
we computed is better

133
00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:38.550
than our previous loss,
We will keep track of that

134
00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:39.770
as the best matrix so far.

135
00:05:40.470 --> 00:05:41.650
And then we back propagate.

136
00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:45.700
And let's run our training loop.

137
00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:50.020
And you can see it's very, very
fast, because again, this

138
00:05:50.020 --> 00:05:52.640
is exactly the same
thing as if we were training a single

139
00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:54.680
linear layer of a
traditional neural network. So

140
00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:56.240
let's take a look at the best
loss that we got.

141
00:05:56.640 --> 00:05:59.180
This is pretty good. A loss of 0.5
is pretty good. It means we've got

142
00:05:59.180 --> 00:06:02.200
pretty much a halfway
improvement in terms of

143
00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:03.180
where we started from.

144
00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:06.480
So one thing we'd like to take a look
at as how the adapter

145
00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:10.300
matrix influences our query vector.
To do that, we

146
00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:13.280
can construct a test vector
consisting of all ones, and

147
00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:16.640
we can multiply that test
vector by our best matrix. And

148
00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:20.380
what this will tell us is which dimensions
of our vectors get

149
00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:23.340
scaled by what amount. You
can think of an embedding adapter

150
00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:27.120
as stretching space
and squeezing space for the dimensions

151
00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:28.540
which are most
relevant to the

152
00:06:28.540 --> 00:06:31.220
particular queries that we have while
reducing the dimensions

153
00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:33.880
that are not relevant to our
query. You'll also notice that it can

154
00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:34.740
reverse dimensions.

155
00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:36.540
So let's plot what that looks like.

156
00:06:36.980 --> 00:06:40.740
And here you can see how
each dimension of our test vector,

157
00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:42.800
which consists only of ones,
has been stretched and squeezed.

158
00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:45.380
Some have been elongated a lot,
while others have been

159
00:06:45.380 --> 00:06:47.460
made to be almost zero.
And so what this

160
00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:49.920
means is our embedding
adapter has basically decided, okay,

161
00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:52.340
these dimensions are more relevant,
these are less relevant.

162
00:06:52.340 --> 00:06:54.880
These are actually opposite
to the things that we want to find,

163
00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:57.240
and these things are actually
more relevant to the things

164
00:06:57.240 --> 00:06:58.040
that we want to find.

165
00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:00.020
Now let's take a look at
what effect this actually

166
00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:00.980
has on our queries.

167
00:07:01.540 --> 00:07:05.160
So let's do as we did before. We'll take
our generated queries and

168
00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:09.000
embed them, and let's compute also
our adapted query embeddings, and then

169
00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:09.780
we project them.

170
00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:14.120
Now let's
plot what we get against our dataset.

171
00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:20.260
And as you can see,
our original queries

172
00:07:20.260 --> 00:07:22.920
were quite scattered around,
but our new queries concentrate in

173
00:07:22.920 --> 00:07:24.960
a certain part of the dataset
which is most relevant to our

174
00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:27.760
queries. You can see how the red
queries have been

175
00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:29.200
adapted through the
embedding adapter to

176
00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:30.620
transform them into
the green queries,

177
00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:32.600
to push them into a
particular part of the space.

178
00:07:32.740 --> 00:07:34.820
So as you can see,
an embedding adapter is a simple

179
00:07:34.820 --> 00:07:37.420
but powerful technique
for customizing query

180
00:07:37.420 --> 00:07:39.800
embeddings to your
specific application.

181
00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:42.140
In order to make this work,
you need to collect a dataset,

182
00:07:42.140 --> 00:07:46.080
either a synthetic one like the one we've
generated here, or else one that's based

183
00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:48.300
on user data. User data
usually works best

184
00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:51.120
because it actually
means that people are using your

185
00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:53.760
retrieval system for
their specific tasks. Again, because

186
00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:55.560
this
approach involves prompting and

187
00:07:55.560 --> 00:07:57.580
because it involves the
use of a large

188
00:07:57.580 --> 00:07:59.800
language model, it's worth experimenting
with the prompts and

189
00:07:59.800 --> 00:08:02.720
it's also worth experimenting with different initializations
of the adapter

190
00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:03.380
matrix.

191
00:08:03.500 --> 00:08:06.540
Even maybe consider using a full
lightweight neural network

192
00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:08.860
and training that instead of a
simple matrix instead.

193
00:08:08.860 --> 00:08:10.900
You might want to tune the
hyperparameters of the

194
00:08:10.900 --> 00:08:14.060
embedding adapter training
process or you might want to

195
00:08:14.060 --> 00:08:16.940
collect more specific data and try
this out with

196
00:08:16.940 --> 00:08:20.160
a specific application
in mind rather than our very

197
00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:22.560
general one of trying to understand
a financial statement.

198
00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:26.240
In the next lesson we'll cover some other
techniques which are just now emerging

199
00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:28.780
from research to
improve embedding-based retrieval

200
00:08:28.780 --> 00:08:29.340
systems.
